**Problem 1 · hard**

Shortest remaining time first is SJF with preemption: whenever a job arrives or finishes, the job with the least remaining time gets the CPU.

| Job | Arrival (ms) | Burst (ms) |
|---|---|---|
| P1 | 0 | 8 |
| P2 | 1 | 5 |
| P3 | 3 | 2 |
| P4 | 6 | 3 |

<solution>Timeline: P1 0 to 1, P2 1 to 3, P3 3 to 5, P2 5 to 8, P4 8 to 11, P1 11 to 18. Completion: P1 18, P2 8, P3 5, P4 11. Waiting = completion − arrival − burst: P1 10, P2 2, P3 0, P4 2. Average 14 / 4 = 3.5 ms.</solution>
<check lang="python">
jobs = {'P1': (0, 8), 'P2': (1, 5), 'P3': (3, 2), 'P4': (6, 3)}
rem = {n: b for n, (a, b) in jobs.items()}
t, done = 0, {}
while rem:
    ready = [n for n in rem if jobs[n][0] <= t]
    n = min(ready, key=lambda n: rem[n])
    rem[n] -= 1
    t += 1
    if rem[n] == 0:
        del rem[n]
        done[n] = t
wait = {n: done[n] - a - b for n, (a, b) in jobs.items()}
assert done == {'P3': 5, 'P2': 8, 'P4': 11, 'P1': 18}
assert wait == {'P1': 10, 'P2': 2, 'P3': 0, 'P4': 2} and sum(wait.values()) / 4 == 3.5
</check>

<ask type="problem">The timeline, each job's waiting time, and the average waiting time.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
At 0 only P1 is there so it runs. At 1 P2 arrives with 5 and P1 has 7 left, so P2 preempts. At 3 P3 arrives with 2 and P2 has 3 left, so P3 preempts and runs 3 to 5. At 5 it's P2 (3 left) against P1 (7 left) so P2 runs; at 6 P4 arrives with 3 but P2 only has 2 left, so P2 keeps going and finishes at 8. Then P4 runs 8 to 11 and P1 runs 11 to 18. Completion times: P1 18, P2 8, P3 5, P4 11. Waiting times: P1 = 18 - 0 - 8 = 10, P2 = 8 - 5 = 3, P3 = 5 - 3 - 2 = 0, P4 = 11 - 6 - 3 = 2. Average = (10 + 3 + 0 + 2) / 4 = 3.75 ms.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.7">The timeline and all four completion times are right, including the hard call at 6: "P2 only has 2 left, so P2 keeps going". The first wrong step is "P2 = 8 - 5 = 3": the arrival time was left out. P2 arrived at 1, so its wait is 8 − 1 − 5 = 2.</grade>

Your average of 3.75 follows correctly from that 3, so it is the same slip, not a second one. With P2 at 2 the waits are 10, 2, 0 and 2, and the average is 14 / 4 = 3.5 ms. You applied the full formula to the other three jobs; P1 hides the omission because its arrival is 0.

<card>Waiting time of a job that arrives after time 0? :: completion − arrival − burst. Leaving out the arrival counts time before the job existed as waiting.</card>

<note>Waiting time = completion − arrival − burst, for every job. A check: no job can wait longer than its completion time minus its arrival time.</note>

